= Introduction

Libtt is a shared library that makes a Tenstorrent  processor @blackhole look, to JAX @jax and to PyTorch @paszke2019, like a TPU. A supported program that calls `jax.jit` or `torch.compile` or uses eager execution runs on the chip without modifications. The library is loaded through PJRT, the interface by which frameworks built on the XLA compiler talk to a device @xla. It holds everything needed beneath that interface: a compiler from StableHLO @stablehlo, the set of tensor operations in which those frameworks hand a program over, to programs for the chip; the run-time system that loads and starts those programs on multiple chips; and the programs' kernels (@stack).

The design goal is that existing model code runs unchanged, and that good performance comes out of the box. Rather than asking users to adapt their models to the hardware, libtt places the work of making a model fast in the compiler and the run-time system: the compiler decides how operations are lowered, fused, and mapped onto the chip, while the run-time system manages program loading, data movement, and execution. Because these optimizations operate on StableHLO rather than on any particular model, they are not tied to the models used to develop them. They apply across models whose operations libtt supports.

