We demonstrate this design on large language model training and serving. TorchTitan's TPU trainer shards and trains models across multiple chips. Two existing serving engines run on libtt: SGLang-JAX @sglangjax, written in JAX, and SGLang-TorchTPU @sglangtorchtpu, written in PyTorch for Google's TPUs. Neither was written for Tenstorrent hardware. SGLang-JAX gains a Tenstorrent backend that calls libtt's kernels for attention and a few other operations through custom calls, while SGLang-TorchTPU runs without source changes. Together they serve the Qwen3 family @qwen3 and its successors, covering dense models, mixture-of-experts models, and hybrids that combine attention with recurrent layers. Training results are in @training; serving results are in @measure and @testing.
We modeled the software stack on the TPU's because the two architectures share the properties that matter most to the compiler (see @accelerator-comparison). Both compute on tiles, so most operations require tensors to be stored in tiled layouts in accelerator memory, and both connect chips with high-speed interconnects that favor mesh-style partitioning across devices.

Libtt builds on Tenstorrent's PJRT plugin @ttxla, MLIR compiler @ttmlir, and tt-metal runtime system @ttmetal. These projects provide the compiler, tensor operations, driver, and kernels, but offer very limited compatibility with existing JAX and PyTorch programs. Libtt addresses these gaps by redesigning parts of the stack, fixing bugs, and adding missing functionality. These changes broaden framework compatibility and integrate the components into a single, capable runtime for both JAX and PyTorch (@history, @library, @compiler, @frameworks, @testing). Libtt's contributions are:
+ *One drop-in runtime for JAX and PyTorch.* A self-contained PJRT library bundles the compiler, runtime, firmware, and kernel sources, and supports PyTorch through the existing `torch_tpu` backend. Applications use their framework APIs without the need to rewrite them using the Python bindings of Tenstorrent's TTNN library (@library, @frameworks).
+ *Wide framework coverage.* General indexing kernels and extensions to reductions, sorting, and control flow make a wide range of JAX and PyTorch programs executable. Compatibility is checked against the upstream JAX and `torch_tpu` test suites (@compiler, @testing) and by running existing training and serving workloads.
+ *Numerical fidelity across the stack.* FP16 and the E4M3FN and E5M2 FP8 formats retain their types through buffers, compilation, and execution; data movement preserves bit patterns and supported matrix products read FP8 operands natively. A vector-unit matrix product provides FP32 arithmetic when highest precision is requested, and integer collectives and cumulative sums preserve exact integer values (@numerical-types, @chips). In addition, many small numerical precision bugs were fixed.
+ *Multi-chip execution.* Tensor partitioning and collectives support both JAX's single-process model and PyTorch's one-process-per-chip model (@chips). To enable this, we redesigned the lower-level communication primitives to support the multiple-program, multiple-data execution model often used in PyTorch.
+ *Optimized from compiler to kernels.* Compiler fusions, row-aware kernels, weight preparation, and traces reduce program launches and data movement. We also implemented conditional traces to support conditional execution without host synchronization (@compiler, @running). In addition, we implemented many optimizations for existing kernels and implemented new kernels to execute operations more efficiently.
+ *Compatibility with existing serving and training software.* The stack supports SGLang-JAX, SGLang-TorchTPU, and TorchTitan's TPU training path. Adapters expose Tenstorrent's fused attention, loss, and mixture-of-experts kernels. Training improvements reduce the memory used by gradients, optimizer steps, and loss computation. Libtt's recurrent kernels maintain state across tokens and support speculative decoding (@training, @serving).

We begin with the hardware and its upstream software foundations (@machine, @history). We then describe libtt's design: the shared library, the compiler, the runtime, and execution across multiple chips (@library to @chips). Next we evaluate the stack on real workloads, covering framework integration, training, and language-model serving with performance results (@frameworks, @training, @serving). We close with framework compatibility results (@testing) and a conclusion with directions for follow-up work (@conclusion). The implementation is available in the project repository @libtt.